Unix environment configuration files

The Unix environment of Hexagonix(1) presents a series of configuration or data files, which can
be kept together in the /etc directory. Those files serve as a database for utilities in
the Unix environment, as well can be used to configure the general behavior of the system.
These files can also be used to expose relevant system information to users, such as
version, edition, status, etc. Some files are important for the functioning of the system,
such as:

- rc: 'init' configuration file. Used to describe the services that must be initialized on every
system boot. Normally, it should not be changed. It is recommended that any comments should be
after the # character.
- shadow: contains the database of users and their related data. This file can be used by the
'login', 'sudo' and 'su' utilities, as well as others that use login routines for
authentication. It is recommended that any comments should be after the # character.
- host: file that contains the hostname of the machine. This file can be changed freely by
users. To do this, just remove the original file and create a new one with the same name,
containing only a name ending with a blank space. Comments cannot be added.
- fstab: mount table read by 'mount -a' at boot. Each line names a device, the directory it
is mounted at, the file system and its options, in that order. It is recommended that any
comments should be after the # character.
- sudoers: list of the users authorized to run 'sudo', one numeric user id per line. A user
absent from it cannot run anything through 'sudo'. It is recommended that any comments should
be after the # character.
- protect: list of the paths 'protect -a' marks as protected at boot, one per line. A path may
be followed by the word setuid to also be marked setuid. It is recommended that any comments
should be after the # character.
- hexgnix.cfg: This file exposes the user to relevant system information, such as version,
update package, build code, release name, etc. It carries one key=value pair per line, and
comments must be after the # character.

More of the system at:

(1) 'man system': more about the Hexagonix.

